{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1499108-why-tudor-london-is-a-great-requiem-setting","id":1499108,"title":"Why Tudor London is a great Requiem-Setting","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-07T19:15:06-08:00","id":1499108,"title":"Why Tudor London is a great Requiem-Setting","content":"1. It rained a lot during that time. The last years of Bloody Mary's reign saw frequent rain, which often interfered with the burning of heretics.<br /> 2. Henry the VIII abolished the Catholic-Church; that means, a lot of abandoned building were throughout London and England.<br /> 3. Change from agriculture to pastoralism meant a lot of farmers and laborers would out of work and flocked to the large-towns and cites, like London. The street were full of the poor and they had no where to go because the churches and monasteries were closed. <br /> 4. Everyone talked like a pirate. <br /> 5. Loads of religious-persecutions; people were being burned alive for believe in what they previous king or queen believed in and refusing to believe in that the previous king or queen would have burned you alive for believing in. <br /> 6. Mortals slept long-hours at night and would often awake, pray and perhaps fornicate and move about at night. <br /> <br /> One negative side-effect were ruffs; it's hard to suck blood from the neck when everyone* is wearing one of those; perhaps an anti-vampire response. <br /> <br /> *Those who could afford one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T15:17:00-08:00","id":1499172,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I'm more just glad the CofD mostly stayed away from doing Everything: the Dark Ages. Dark Eras springs around everywhere, although it seems that the writers really didn't have much inspiration for Vampire.<br /> <br /> I find it interesting to see 'everybody talked like a pirate' as a thing, to my knowledge everybody sounded disappointingly American. What's the point of playing English vampires if I can't do a ridiculous RP accent :P<br /> <br /> As to the riffs, honestly I suspect that even in the modern day many vampires avoid the neck. Back then it was due to the ruff, today it's because it's a well known trope. I imagine there was a lot of feeding from the wrists or similar.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I think it's biggest issue is probably one shared by Requiem for Rome: the resulting setup is just a bit too close to baseline Requiem. Yes there are changes, but the most interesting Dark Eras were ones which either changed up the formula a lot or revealed more about the mythology of splats (The Sundered World really sheds new light on Uratha legends and beliefs). Requiem for Regina isn't bad, but it's not particularly exciting.<br /> <br /> Plus you just know that some player is going to embrace Shakespeare.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:blue\">Blue is sarcasm.</span><br /> <br /> If I suggestion I make contradicts in-setting metaphysics please ignore me, I probably brought in scientific ideas.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4507-twodsix","name":"TwoDSix","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"833"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T20:43:53-08:00","id":1499187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwoDSix</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1499108-why-tudor-london-is-a-great-requiem-setting?p=1499172#post1499172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I'm more just glad the CofD mostly stayed away from doing Everything: the Dark Ages. Dark Eras springs around everywhere, although it seems that the writers really didn't have much inspiration for Vampire.<br /> <br /> I find it interesting to see 'everybody talked like a pirate' as a thing, to my knowledge everybody sounded disappointingly American. What's the point of playing English vampires if I can't do a ridiculous RP accent :P . . .<br /> <br /> Honestly, I think it's biggest issue is probably one shared by Requiem for Rome: the resulting setup is just a bit too close to baseline Requiem. Yes there are changes, but the most interesting Dark Eras were ones which either changed up the formula a lot or revealed more about the mythology of splats (The Sundered World really sheds new light on Uratha legends and beliefs). Requiem for Regina isn't bad, but it's not particularly exciting.<br /> <br /> Plus you just know that some player is going to embrace Shakespeare.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dark Eras reminds me of AD&amp;D 2e's Blue Book, Creative-Campaigning; Everything Dark Ages was, um, OK. I like the Middle-Ages, but, Dark Ages, was a bit over done; too much focus on a mega-plot when most stories should revolve around a few vampires in a country-town. Though, Mage<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />A was good. Different. <br /> <br /> Any Requiem game should take place in the past, because; how else are you going to play off immortality? <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}